{
  "kind": "tea",
  "teaId": "mary-perfume",
  "text": "She poured a year's wages onto his feet in a room full of people doing the math out loud.\n\nJohn twelve. Six days before Passover, in Bethany. Supper. Martha's serving, Lazarus is at the table.\n\nAnd Mary takes a pound of ointment of spikenard, very costly, and anoints the feet of Jesus, and wipes his feet with her hair.\n\nAnd the house was filled with the odour of the ointment. Everybody in that room knows exactly what just happened, because they can smell it.\n\nThen Judas speaks up. And he uses the language of compassion. \"Why was not this ointment sold for three hundred pence, and given to the poor?\"\n\nThree hundred pence. Going by a penny being a day's wage elsewhere in the Gospels, that's roughly a year of work. Poured out on the floor.\n\nSo on the face of it, it's not a stupid question.\n\nExcept John tells us the motive outright. \"This he said, not that he cared for the poor, but because he was a thief, and had the bag.\"\n\nHe was skimming.\n\nAnd Jesus does not let her sit there and take it. \"Let her alone. Against the day of my burying hath she kept this.\"\n\nHe defended her out loud, in the room, in front of the man counting.",
